        <section id="feedback-in-agile-and-the-scrum-framework"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Feedback in Agile and the Scrum framework</h2>
              <p>Scroll through the slides below to learn more about where productive feedback is used in Agile development and the Scrum framework.</p>

              
              
<div class="gallery-container">
  <div role="group" class="gallery no-js">
    <ul>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Feedback in the 'Sprint Review'
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>The sprint ‘Review’ is where a demonstration of the product is given to the wider team and stakeholders in a meeting at the end of a sprint. This is one of the main areas of the Scrum framework where feedback is given and is important.</p>
<p>The review Feedback is encouraged and helps the team to determine: if the product is meeting set criteria, fit for purpose, and for identification of areas of change or improvement.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/uc/uc_u5_s2_p2_slide1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Feedback in the 'Sprint Retrospective'
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>The Retrospective is another aspect of Scrum where feedback is important – it is feedback from the current sprint in as what went right, what went wrong, learnings, and actions.  It is a safe space for sharing – one common term is ‘what is said in the Retro stays in the retro’ – so there is no blame, judgements, or blatant negativity and it is advised that things are kept light but focused.</p>
<p>We will be taking a much more detailed look at Agile development and the Scrum framework in Session 4.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/uc/uc_u5_s2_p2_slide2.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
